xt-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">11. State Persistence</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Final textual response and accumulated turn artifacts.</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Conversational turn committed to SQLite hermes_state.py; memory changes flushed to MEMORY.md and US[span_115](start_span)[span_115](end_span)ER.md.</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Persistent state written atomically to disk; gateway auto-compression fires if context exceeds 85%.</span></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1" style="border-right-style:solid;border-bottom-color:#000000;border-top-width:1pt;border-right-width:1pt;border-left-color:#000000;vertical-align:top;border-right-color:#000000;border-left-width:1pt;border-top-style:solid;border-left-style:solid;border-bottom-width:1pt;width:93.6pt;border-top-color:#000000;border-bottom-style:solid"><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Session persisted; final response delivered to originating client surface.</span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The stage mechanics begin with objective ingestion, where the runtime binds profile roots and assigns a unique task_id. During prompt assembly, prompt_builder.py segments context into stable, contextual, and volatile blocks to ensure Anthropic and OpenAI prefix cache preservation. Before any external model call is dispatched, the preflight audit evaluates conversation length; if the context exceeds 50% of the maximum window, [span_363](start_span)[span_363](end_span)[span_366](start_span)[span_366](end_span)context_compressor.py executes a lossy summarization of intermediate turns while preserving the final 20 messages and all active system blocks intact.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Provider calls execute within _interruptible_[span_118](start_span)[span_118](end_span)api_call(), which runs the HTTP request in a worker thread. This prevents system hangs and enables immediate cancellation without corrupting session history if an operator aborts the run. When a model outputs tool calls, Hermes splits execution: internal state tools (tod[span_121](start_span)[span_121](end_span)o, memory, session_search, delegate_task) are intercepted directly by run_age[span_122](start_span)[span_122](end_span)nt.py, while standard system tools pass through model_tools.py to the tool registry.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The tool registry enforces environment gating via registered check_fn callbacks, filtering out tools whose underlying binaries or API keys are missing. If a command executes through terminal_tool, it passes through tools/approval.py, where 24 destructive regex patterns are evaluated. Non-interactive environments (such as headless cron daemons) reject dangerous operations that have not been explicitly whitelisted in config.yaml. Tool dispatch then handles single commands on the main thread and multiple commands concurrently via ThreadPoolExecutor, while interactive tools such as clarify force sequential execution.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Outputs and exceptions are captured without raising runtime errors; handlers format failures into structured JSON strings ({"error": "message"}), pass the text through agent/redact.py to scrub credentials, and insert them into conversation history. Results are strictly sorted back into their original call sequence, enforcing OpenAI-compatible sequence rules. The loop audits the IterationBudget, returning to preflight inspection for subsequent turns until the model produces a final text completion, at which point messages are committed to SQLite (hermes_state.py) and memory markdown files are flushed to disk.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Capability Boundaries Across the Architecture Waterfall</span></h2><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Establishing an autonomous production architecture requires separating native Hermes primitives from capabilities that necessitate external extensions, skills, or custom software builds.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Clearly Native Capabilities</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The following capabilities reside entirely within the core Hermes repository and require no external skills, MCP servers, or custom wrapper code:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_1-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Multi-Backend Terminal Substrate</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Complete process management across local environments, Docker containers, SSH endpoints, Singularity, Modal, Daytona, and Vercel Sandbox backends via tools/terminal_tool.py.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Native Browser Automation Suite</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: 10 dedicated browser tools (browser_navigate, browser_snapshot, browser_click, browser_type, browser_scroll, browser_back, browser_press, browse[span_127](start_span)[span_127](end_span)r_dialog, browser_cdp, browser_vision) driven locally by agent-browser or attached to an active Chrome DevTools Protocol port.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Programmatic Tool Calling (PTC)</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Internal RPC execution via execute_code, allowing an LLM to author Python scripts that call registered tools over Unix sockets to collapse multi-step loops.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Multi-Agent Kanban Coordination</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Task state machine, dependency graphs, OS worker process management, and automated review cycles implemented natively in hermes_cli/kanban_db.py and tools/kanban_tools.py.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Ephemeral In-Process Delegation</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Spawning isolated child subagents with distinct conversational contexts and independent iteration limits via delegate_task.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Fuzzy-Matched File Operations</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Paginated line-indexed file reading, file creation, regex search, and 9-strategy fuzzy find-and-replace patching via tools/file_tools.py.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Unattended Cron Automation</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Built-in scheduling engine (cron/scheduler.py) capable of running recurring jobs with shell pre-run scripts, prompt execution, and cross-platform notification dispatch.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Persistent Context and Memory Tiers</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Profile-isolated markdown stores (MEMORY.md, USER.md), SQLite conversation logs with FTS5 indexing, and dynamic context compression.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Security Pattern Guardrails</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Interception of 24 destructive shell patterns via tools/approval.py with session and permanent allowlists.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Extension Required Capabilities</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The following operations cannot be executed by Hermes natively and require external skills, plugins, MCP servers, or external services:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_2-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Video Composition and Rendering Engines</span><span>: Hermes possesses no native image stitching, video rendering, or timeline manipulation code. Driving FFmpeg, Remotion, MoviePy, or Blender requires terminal scripts or custom tool plugins. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">(Extension-level capability; investigate in Research Pass 2/3).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Audio Digital Signal Processing and Voice Synthesis</span><span>: Native Hermes code cannot synthesize speech or balance audio tracks. Voice generation requires connecting to external APIs (e.g., ElevenLabs, OpenAI Audio) or local voice synthesis engines via MCP or CLI. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">(Extension-level capability; investigate in Research Pass 2/3).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">YouTube Data API Publishing and Analytics</span><span>: Hermes contains no native integration with Google OAuth or YouTube Studio endpoints. Publishing videos, managing metadata, and polling YouTube Analytics requires dedicated API scripts or MCP servers. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">(Extension-level capability; investigate in Research Pass 2/3).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Dialectic Knowledge Graph Modeling</span><span>: While Hermes supports flat markdown memory natively, advanced semantic graphs and dialectic user modeling require external memory provider plugins such as Honcho. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">(Extension-level capability; investigate in Research Pass 2/3).</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">GitHub Advanced Collaboration Automation</span><span>: Repository triage and pull request automation require installing the optional GitHub skill or authenticating the external gh CLI. </span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">(Extension-level capability; investigate in Research Pass 2/3).</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Unclear Capabilities</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Capabilities where documentation implies functionality, but operational stability or boundaries remain ambiguous:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_3-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Cross-Profile Concurrent Database Access</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Whether multiple independent Hermes profile daemons can simultaneously perform heavy read/write operations against a shared SQLite Kanban database without encountering database locks (sqlite3.OperationalError: database is locked) is unverified under high-concurrency loads.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Daemon Process Survival Across Sandbox Reaping</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: The documentation explicitly notes that cloud sandbox backends preserve disk files but do not guarantee that background processes spawned with background=true survive idle reaping or container restarts.</span></li></ul><h3 style="padding-top:12pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Not Established Capabilities</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Capabilities for which no credible evidence exists in the codebase:</span></p><ul class="lst-kix_list_4-0 start" style="padding:0;margin:0"><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Distributed Multi-Host Consensus</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: Hermes does not support distributed agent cluster consensus protocols (such as Raft or Paxos); multi-agent coordination is strictly local-host or client-server.</span></li><li style="padding:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.0;margin-right:0;margin-left:30pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0;text-align:left"><span style="font-style:italic">Automatic Transactional Filesystem Rollback</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">: If a task fails or is aborted halfway through execution, Hermes provides no native mechanism to revert mutated files or rollback workspace directories to a clean git commit; all recovery must be explicitly scripted.</span></li></ul><h2 style="padding-top:11.2pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Critical Operational Limitations and Edge-Case Vulnerabilities</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Auditing the Hermes Agent codebase, architectural documentation, and issue tracker reveals specific operational limitations and failure modes that impact autonomous production systems.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;HERMES CONTROL PLANE FAILURE MODES &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br>| IN-MEMORY PLANNING VOLATILITY &nbsp; &nbsp; | tools/todo_tool.py stores task states in an &nbsp; |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; [span_298](start_span)[span_298](end_span)[span_301](start_span)[span_301](end_span) &nbsp; | unpersisted array; process restart caus[span_247](start_span)[span_247](end_span)[span_249](start_span)[span_249](end_span)es &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| complete loss of active planning state. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br>| SCHEDULER PROFILE ISOLATION GAPS &nbsp; | cronjob tool under -p writes to profile dir; |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| gateway daemon reads root jobs.json; profile |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| scheduled tasks are silently orphaned. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br>| CONTAINER CONFIGURATION DRIFT &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| terminal_tool honors docker_forward_env; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| file_tools and code_execution drop env keys; |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| sandboxes silently lose credentials/network. |<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br>| SUBPROCESS PARSE &amp; PATH FAILURES &nbsp; | Background rewrite omits bash separator &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| before echo PID; lifecycle guard crashes on &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| venv paths due to embedded null bytes. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br>| RUNTIME CONCURRENCY CRASHES &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| Python 3.14 breaks DaemonThreadPoolExecutor &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| internals; breaks delegate_task, async &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;| delegation, and parallel tool dispatch. &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------+<br></span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">In-Memory Planning Volatility</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The native planning tool (tools/todo_tool.py) manages decomposition lists entirely in memory via an unpersisted class instance (TodoStore with self._items = []). State is not flushed to state.db or written to a backing JSON file. If the host process restarts, crashes, or reboots during a long-running video assembly sequence, all active todo items are wiped. For an autonomous pipeline, task states must never be entrusted to todo_tool; orchestration must instead rely on the SQLite-backed Kanban subsystem.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Scheduler Profile Isolation and Permissions Gaps</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The cron subsystem (cron/scheduler.py) exhibits path divergence when profiles are utilized. If an agent operating inside a specific profile (hermes -p video_director) invokes the cronjob tool, the scheduled entry is written to ~/.hermes/profiles/video_director/cron/jobs.json. However, the background gateway daemon (hermes gateway run), unless explicitly launched with that identical profile, monitors only the root ~/.hermes/cron/jobs.json. The job appears active when queried by the agent, but the gateway scheduler never triggers it. Furthermore, cron/jobs.py forcibly applies POSIX 0600 permissions on every write to jobs.json, which breaks multi-container architectures where a WebUI container and a gateway daemon run under different user identifiers.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Subprocess String Rewriting and Path Evaluation Bugs</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">When running terminal commands in the background (background=true), tools/terminal_tool.py executes _rewrite_trailing_background() to rewrite compound commands of the form A &amp;&amp; B &amp; into { B &amp; }. The rewriter fails to emit a semicolon or space before subsequent appended commands, producing strings such as { B &amp; } echo PID:$!. Bash rejects this with a syntax error, causing detached process spawning and remote kernel initialization to fail on Docker backends. Additionally, the gateway lifecycle guard (cron/lifecycle_guard.py) attempts to inspect terminal commands for dangerous process manipulation. When a command references a script using an interpreter path inside a virtual environment (e.g., /venv/bin/python script.py), the scanner's file resolution logic attempts to read binary files without checking magic bytes, raising an unhandled ValueError: embedded null byte that hard-fails the command.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Container Configuration Drift</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Hermes initializes Docker execution environments through three separate tool modules: tools/terminal_tool.py, tools/file_tools.py, and tools/code_execution_tool.py. While terminal_tool.py correctly populates container_config with keys defined in config.yaml (docker_env, docker_forward_env, docker_extra_args), file_tools.py and code_execution_tool.py construct container dictionaries omitting these keys. If an execution turn begins with a file read or Python code execution before a terminal command is issued, the container is spawned without forwarded environment variables or custom network definitions, causing subsequent authenticated tool operations inside the container to fail.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Runtime Concurrency Incompatibilities</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">The threading substrate used for concurrent tool dispatch and subagent delegation (DaemonThreadPoolExecutor) directly manipulates internal attributes of Python's standard library ThreadPoolExecutor. Under Python 3.14+, the standard library refactored worker context initialization, removing _initializer and _initargs. Running Hermes on Python 3.14 causes delegate_task, asynchronous tool fan-out, and memory synchronization to crash immediately with an unhandled AttributeError. Autonomous production deployments must pin host environments strictly to Python 3.11 or 3.12.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Kanban Dispatcher Auto-Promotion Loops</span></h3><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Within the Kanban engine, tasks blocked pending external verification (kanban_block(reason="review-required: ...")) historically encountered a promotion bug in hermes_cli/kanban_db.py. The dispatcher treated worker blocks identically to worker crashes, re-promoting the task back to ready after a tick interval. The respawned worker inspected the disk, observed that the implementation was already present, and exited cleanly without issuing a terminal completion command. The dispatcher flagged this clean zero-exit as a protocol_violation and repeatedly respawned the worker until the retry budget was exhausted, burning API tokens in a tight loop.</span></p><h2 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:18pt;padding-bottom:11.2pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:18pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Architectural Mapping for Autonomous Video Production</span></h2><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">While Hermes cannot compose video frames natively, its verified control-plane capabilities allow it to function as an effective Master Production Director. The following architectural mapping aligns native Hermes subsystems to the requirements of an automated content pipeline.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Courier&quot;;font-style:normal">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;HERMES MASTER PRODUCTION DIRECTOR &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>| &nbsp;* AIAgent Synchronous Loop (Strict Sequence Invariants) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp;* Persistent SQLite Kanban Dispatcher Engine &amp; Board State &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; |<br>| &nbsp;* Programmatic Tool Calling (PTC) via Unix RPC Socket &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>| &nbsp;* Headless Cron Scheduler Daemon &amp; Platform Notification Gateway &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;|<br>+---------------------------------------+-------------------------------------